 "title": "Writing number sentences",
 "audience": "learner",
 "keyIdea": "Write calculations as expressions, and read what an expression tells you without working it out.",
 "explain": {
  "kid": [
   "A number sentence records a calculation. 'Three bags of 12 apples, plus 5 loose apples' is 3 × 12 + 5.",
   "You can compare expressions without working them out. 3 × (18,932 + 921) is three times as big as 18,932 + 921, because it is three lots of the same thing."
  ],
  "adult": [
   "Writing an expression means recording the structure of a calculation, not just its answer: 'add 8 and 7, then multiply by 2' is 2 × (8 + 7).",
   "Reading expressions without calculating is a key step towards algebra. You can see that 3 × (18,932 + 921) is three times 18,932 + 921, and that 500 − 199 is one more than 500 − 200."
  ]
 },
